// STAGE 4 - PLACE + DEFORM (spec 4.4), THE DEFORM GATE.  13.9 N5.
//   POINT wrangle over PLAN POINTS, precision 64.
//   input 0  the plan, carrying `pc_proto`'s module numbers AND, since 13.9 N6,
//            that module's own station table (`_st` / `_st_ax`)
//   input 1  CONFIG
//   input 2  the DECOMPOSED spline (the sampler table)
//   input 3  IN_SURFACE - 4.5's drape (13.9 N6)
//
// ⚠️ INPUT 3 USED TO BE `pc_kit_meta` AND THE MOVE IS 13.9 N6's WIRING.  A
// wrangle has four inputs and this node already used all four, so the station
// table travels on the PLAN POINT now (`pc_proto` writes it, from the kit it
// already holds) and the port is free for the surface.  Nothing else changed
// about the table: same raw local x, same `- ax` in 64 bits here.
//
// `place._needs_deform` ported: ONE rule, in ONE place, deciding whether a
// piece ships as a packed instance or as rebuilt geometry.  It is the single
// most expensive thing left in the Python kernel on the shape citygen and
// PC-G3 actually run - profiled on the 20 km fence, `_needs_deform` plus
// `span_deviation` is 0.133 s of `place.build`'s 0.813 s, more than the
// fitting solve and the packed transform put together - and it is what
// decides whether 10 005 pieces cost 0.42 s and 12 MB or 21.9 s and 360 180
// points (D69).
//
// THE REFERENCE'S ORDER, KEPT EXACTLY, because every line of it is a decision
// somebody had to make twice:
//
//   1. a SLICED piece or one carrying a corner cut always deforms (D41 - a
//      miter unpacks). `pc_sliced` is the plan's own flag; a corner cut
//      cannot reach here at all, because 4.3 is not ported and a case with
//      corners is out of scope by name.
//   2. an ANCHORED piece never does - it is a straight corner leg. Anchors
//      come only from `corner.py`, so the same sentence applies.
//   3. `pc_deform <= 0` never does (D27) - the module said it is rigid.
//   4. a D99 BAND with ground under it always does - a band is a per-point
//      rule and a packed prim is one matrix. NOT PORTED, and this node
//      DECLARES ITSELF UNANSWERABLE rather than guessing (see below).
//   5. the CURVATURE BUDGET (D75/D87): `span_deviation > bend_tol`.
//   6. 4.5's drape test - a dead-straight spline over a ridge has no interior
//      vertex, so the budget above says "nothing to follow". Needs the
//      conform (N6), and the same declaration covers it.
//   7. `vertical`: a SHEARED span deforms, whatever the plan curve did.
//
// ⚠️ AND WHAT IT REFUSES TO ANSWER IS AN ATTRIBUTE, NOT A COMMENT.  Steps 4
// and 6 need a band and a surface this graph has not ported, and a gate that
// silently answered "packed" for them would be the exact failure the rebuild
// is most at risk of: a green suite over a tool that quietly stopped
// segregating.  `pc_frame_valid` already carries `pc_proto`'s refusal for a
// surface, a fillet, a slope fix and D98's flatten; this adds D99's band to
// it, so an unanswerable piece is DROPPED by `pc_place_valid` exactly as
// those are, and `Stage = output` keeps the reference for that build.
//
// ⚠️ 13.9 N5 LANDED THE DEFORMED BRANCH, SO THIS NODE'S VERDICT IS NO LONGER
// A DEAD END.  `pc_deformed = 1` used to mean "this piece leaves the native
// branch and the whole build goes to the reference"; it now means "this piece
// is built by `copy_deformed` + `pc_deform`".  Three things that were somebody
// else's problem while every deformed piece was refused are this node's
// problem now, and each is a REFUSAL rather than a guess:
//
//   * D25's `_bend_deviation` - the one warning only a DEFORMED piece can
//     raise.  It is computed here (`pc_bend_deviation`, pc_gate.h) because
//     this is the node that already holds the span, the verdict and, since
//     N5, the module's station table on input 3.  Without it the native
//     branch ships an element the reference stamps `pc_warn_bend_resolution`
//     on and it does not, which `output_guard_parity` reads as a missing
//     PRIM ATTRIBUTE on the whole build.
//   * D31's frame TRANSPORT is a prefix scan over the piece's stations, which
//     a per-point wrangle cannot evaluate in O(1).  `pc_frames_transportable`
//     asks whether the flip is reachable at all over this span and refuses
//     the piece where it is - see pc_gate.h for why the sample set is sound.
//   * a SLICED piece (`fill = tile`) needs 4.6's slice caps, which is N7.
//     Level 1 already refuses a tile fill outright, so this is the assertion
//     of that and not a second implementation of it - but a gate that
//     depended on another node's refusal without saying so is how an unported
//     feature gets shipped the day that other refusal is widened.

#include "pc_gate.h"

int    pr    = i@pc_curveprim;
float  sa    = f@pc_ws0;
float  sb    = f@pc_ws1;
string zmode = s@pc_zmode;

dict cfg = detail(1, "pc_cfg");
float tol = 0.01;
if (isvalidindex(cfg, "bend_tol")) { float v = cfg["bend_tol"]; tol = v; }
string band_side = "";
if (isvalidindex(cfg, "flat_band")) { string v = cfg["flat_band"]; band_side = v; }
float band_m = 0.0;
if (isvalidindex(cfg, "flat_band_m")) { float v = cfg["flat_band_m"]; band_m = v; }
// 13.9 N6 - 4.5's drop axis (D51). It is a DIRECTION, not an axis menu: a
// wall-mounted run conforms sideways. `Params.conform_axis` normalises it, so
// this is a unit vector already; the fallback is the parm's own default.
vector caxis = set(0.0, -1.0, 0.0);
if (isvalidindex(cfg, "conform_axis")) { vector v = cfg["conform_axis"]; caxis = v; }
// the surface input, as ONE number every sampler call below carries. An
// unwired input, or one with no primitive, reads back as `pc_sample` itself -
// the same call, so no build without a surface can move.
int surf = pc_surf_active(3) ? 3 : -1;
// ...and published for `pc_deform_prep`, which has no CONFIG input of its own.
v@_caxis = caxis;

// `place._band` returns None for exactly these three reasons, and this is
// that test rather than a re-derivation of it: no side chosen, a zero height,
// or an `adaptive` piece (it rides the full frame, so it has no flat half to
// hold). Whether the band reaches into the module is `_band`'s fourth test
// and it does not change the answer here - `_needs_deform` only asks WHETHER
// there is a band, and a band that swallows the module is still a band.
int has_band = ((band_side == "top" || band_side == "bottom")
                && band_m > PC_EPS && zmode != "adaptive");

// D87: the budget is spent by the piece's WORST POINT, not by its spine.
// `adaptive` rolls y and z with the frame, a yaw-only mode keeps y world-
// vertical, so the reach off the spine is the full radius in one case and the
// z half in the other.
float radius = (zmode == "adaptive") ? f@_proto_radius : f@_proto_rz;
// the module's nominal fitted length, hoisted above the gate because 13.9 N6's
// `fracs` are measured against it and the reference's `_Proto.fracs` is.
float plen = f@pc_proto_len;

vector a, ta, b, tb;
pc_cspan_ends(2, pr, sa, sb, surf, caxis, a, ta, b, tb);

// 13.9 N6 - the piece's own stations, as FRACTIONS of its fitted length, which
// is `_Proto.fracs` exactly: `(st[i] - ax) / length`. D71 - they are what 4.5's
// two probes sample on, so the conform gate and the conform deform read the
// same ground by construction rather than by agreement.
float st[] = f[]@_st;
float st_ax = f@_st_ax;
float fracs[];
if (plen > PC_EPS)
    foreach (float x; st) push(fracs, (x - st_ax) / plen);

float dev = 0.0;
int needs = 0;
if (i@pc_sliced) {
    needs = 1;
} else if (i@pc_deform > 0) {
    dev = pc_span_deviation(2, pr, sa, sb, radius, zmode, surf, caxis);
    if (dev > tol) {
        needs = 1;
    // 4.5: A DEAD-STRAIGHT SPLINE OVER A RIDGE HAS NO INTERIOR VERTEX, so the
    // budget above says "nothing to follow" and a bendable rail crosses the
    // hill as one rigid chord with its two ends on the ground. `deviates`
    // measures the drape against that chord, so a piece on a UNIFORM slope -
    // where the drape IS the chord - still stays packed. `stepped` is excluded
    // because sitting flat is the mode (4.5's own "stepped sits on it").
    } else if (zmode != "stepped"
               && pc_conform_deviates(2, pr, sa, sb, tol, surf, caxis, fracs)) {
        needs = 1;
    } else if (zmode == "vertical") {
        needs = (abs(b.y - a.y) > 1e-6);
    }
}

// --- 13.9 N5: what the DEFORMED branch needs from this node -----------------
//
// `place.build` pass A: `if p.anchor is None and deformed and
// module.deform > 0`, transcribed.  A sliced piece reaches `needs = 1` with
// `pc_deform == 0` and the reference does not measure it either.
int kprim   = i@_proto_kitprim;
// `place.build`'s own `scale` - the SPAN over the module's nominal length,
// which is not `pc_frames`' chord-based fit scale.  The deform maps local x
// onto ARC LENGTH; the packed transform maps it onto the CHORD.
float dscale = (plen > PC_EPS) ? ((sb - sa) / plen) : 1.0;
float bend_dev = 0.0;
if (needs && i@pc_deform > 0 && kprim >= 0)
    bend_dev = pc_bend_deviation(2, pr, st, st_ax, sa, dscale, surf, caxis);
f@_bend_dev = bend_dev;
f@_dscale   = dscale;

// the three refusals above, as one flag.  Only a piece that will actually be
// DEFORMED can trip them: a packed piece takes one chord transform and asks
// none of these questions.
int deform_ok = 1;
if (needs) {
    if (i@pc_sliced) deform_ok = 0;             // 4.6's slice caps - N7
    if (kprim < 0) deform_ok = 0;               // no module, no stations
    // the key `pc_order` sorts the two streams back together on.  A module
    // with more prims or points than one span would collide with the next
    // piece's key, so the bound is CHECKED rather than assumed.
    // ⚠️ TYPED LOCALS BEFORE THE COMPARE - the read returns an untyped value
    // and `>=` is ambiguous over it (this project's recorded trap, hit for the
    // sixth time here).  13.9 N6 - off the PLAN POINT now, because input 3 is
    // IN_SURFACE; `pc_proto` carried both numbers across from `pc_kit_meta`.
    int rank_max = i@_krank_max;
    int rank_span = i@_kspan;
    if (rank_max >= rank_span) deform_ok = 0;
    // ⚠️ THE SPAN THE FRAMES ARE ASKED ABOUT IS THE STATION SPAN, NOT THE
    // PLANNED ONE.  D20: geometry outside [ax, ax + size.x] legitimately
    // OVERHANGS and is carried along, so the last station can sit PAST `sb`
    // and the transport question has to be asked out there too.
    if (deform_ok && kprim >= 0) {
        float shi = sb;
        if (len(st)) shi = max(sb, sa + (st[len(st) - 1] - st_ax) * dscale);
        if (!pc_frames_transportable(2, pr, sa, shi, surf, caxis,
                                     st, st_ax, dscale)) deform_ok = 0;
    }
}
// `_gate_deform_ok`, not `_deform_ok`: `copy_deformed` transfers the
// piece's numbers with the wildcard `_d*`, which the shorter name
// matches, and a diagnostic riding onto a million copied points is a
// cost nobody asked for.
i@_gate_deform_ok = deform_ok;

// 4.6's TWO PLACE-SIDE WARNINGS (D32), raised here because this is the node
// that already holds the span's two ends and the deform verdict they read.
// Both are `place.build` pass A, both are one ratio, and without them a
// build the guard switch hands to the native chain would ship an element the
// reference stamps a warning on and this one does not.
//
//   `_flat_ratio` - D32: a yaw-only mode measures the piece along the
//   horizontal, so a vertical span leaves nothing to scale by. It used to
//   build silently - a 3 m vertical curve produced 25 posts 0.0000 m wide,
//   stacked coincident, with no warning. The onset is continuous (0.0852 m at
//   45 degrees, 0.0021 m at 89) so it is a RATIO and not a test for exactly
//   vertical.
//
//   `_chord_ratio` - the same lesson for a RIGID piece: a suppressed hairpin
//   turns corner-cutting into an order-of-magnitude collapse, a 2.5 m beam
//   asked to cover 4 m of a there-and-back polyline materialising 0.10 m long.
//
// ⚠️ THEY GO INTO THE PIECE'S OWN WARNING STRING, NOT INTO NEW ATTRIBUTES.
// `setpointattrib` with a literal name creates the attribute at RUNTIME, and
// the order two such names are created in a MULTITHREADED wrangle is thread
// order - which would put the output's attribute LIST at the mercy of the
// scheduler while every value stayed right. `pc_warn_collate` fans the string
// out on one thread, exactly as `pc_plan_emit` already does for the plan's
// own warnings and for the same reason (D150).
#define PC_FLAT_RATIO_W    0.01     // place.FLAT_RATIO
#define PC_COLLAPSE_RATIO  0.5      // place.COLLAPSE_RATIO
string warns = s@pc_warns;
// 4.5 / D53 - A MISS KEEPS THE UNPROJECTED POSITION AND SAYS SO, and it is
// raised HERE because this is the node that already holds the span and the
// station table the probe reads.  It covers all three ways to miss with one
// behaviour - a hole in the surface, a run that leaves its edge, and no surface
// under that stretch at all: the fence carries on at spline elevation and the
// warning says where it stopped being draped.
//
// ⚠️ IT SITS BEFORE THE TWO RATIOS BELOW BECAUSE `place.build` APPENDS IT
// THERE.  The order of this string is the order `pc_warn_collate` creates the
// prim attributes in, and the reference's own list is the contract.
//
// ⚠️ AND IT IS PROBED ON THE PIECE'S OWN SPAN AND ITS OWN STATIONS (D71), not
// on the whole run and not on five even samples: a 0.1 m hole under a 2 m panel
// whose stations are 0.25 m apart punched a 0.1875 m V-notch into the rail
// while `pc_warn_conform_miss` stayed absent.
if (pc_conform_missed(2, pr, sa, sb, surf, caxis, fracs)
        && find(warns, "pc_warn_conform_miss") < 0)
    warns = (warns == "") ? "pc_warn_conform_miss"
                          : warns + " pc_warn_conform_miss";
float aspan = abs(sb - sa);
float flat_ratio = 1.0;
if (zmode != "adaptive" && aspan > PC_EPS)
    flat_ratio = length(set(b.x - a.x, 0.0, b.z - a.z)) / aspan;
if (flat_ratio < PC_FLAT_RATIO_W)
    warns = (warns == "") ? "pc_warn_degenerate_frame"
                          : warns + " pc_warn_degenerate_frame";
float chord_ratio = (aspan <= PC_EPS) ? 1.0 : (length(b - a) / aspan);
if (!needs && chord_ratio < PC_COLLAPSE_RATIO
        && find(warns, "pc_warn_corner_degenerate") < 0)
    warns = (warns == "") ? "pc_warn_corner_degenerate"
                          : warns + " pc_warn_corner_degenerate";
// D25 - the DEFORMED piece's own warning, and it is the third one this node
// raises rather than a fourth node.  `place.build` appends it right here in
// pass A, under exactly the condition `bend_dev` was computed under.
if (bend_dev > tol)
    warns = (warns == "") ? "pc_warn_bend_resolution"
                          : warns + " pc_warn_bend_resolution";
s@_warns = warns;

// ⚠ THE ANSWER IS `pc_deformed`, WHICH IS 3.4's OWN NAME FOR IT, AND THAT
// IS NOT A CONVENTION VIOLATION.  13.3.4 asked for a point group called
// `pc_needs_deform`; conventions.md 2 says an internal is `_*` and `_scrub`
// deletes it before OUT - so an `_*` gate answer is invisible at
// `Stage = gate`, which is the one place an artist would go to ask WHY a run
// unpacked. This node computes exactly 3.4's `pc_deformed`, one build stage
// early, so it publishes it under that name and `pc_finalize` writes the same
// attribute on the built prim. `_gate_dev` stays `_*`: the metres are a
// diagnostic, not contract.
f@_gate_dev    = dev;
i@pc_deformed  = needs;
// the two the branch cannot answer, folded into the flag `pc_place_valid`
// already blasts on.  `pc_gate_valid` stays as its own attribute - beside
// `pc_frame_valid`, for the same reason and by the same rule - so a check can
// tell "this piece is packed" from "this piece was never judged".
int gate_valid = (i@pc_frame_valid && !has_band && deform_ok);
i@pc_gate_valid = gate_valid;
if (!gate_valid) i@pc_frame_valid = 0;
if (has_band && @ptnum == 0)
    warning("polyChain: the native deform gate cannot answer a build with a Flat Band - D99's band is a per-point rule and its deformed half needs 4.4's band datum, which is not ported. Set Stage = Output for the full result.");
